[
  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987450",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-28271",
      "knowledge_point_id": "fe83dedc-3034-5e3a-b546-dd57c0ccc6e8",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given an Angle",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987450/assets/7dc806c646f1e9f5edbfef789fbdc9dfca2745c637906bd61eccb8072440e8c0.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to one decimal place, find the area of $△ABC$ shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={63}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘}) & =\\frac{3x+2}{3x} \\\\ 3x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘}) & =3x+2 \\\\ 3x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘})-3x & =2 \\\\ 3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘})-1) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{3(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘})-1)} \\\\ x & =0.692\\,5…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =3x+2=3(0.692\\,5…)+2=4.077\\,6…, \\\\ AC & =3x=3(0.692\\,5…)=2.077\\,6….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}(2.077\\,6…)(4.077\\,6…) \\\\  & =4.236\\,0… \\\\  & ≈4.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the one decimal place.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "6.3",
