    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-105465",
      "knowledge_point_id": "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous",
      "problem": "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{2x+16}$ is continuous.",
      "worked_solution": "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{2x+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{2(x+8)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=-8.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-8)$ and $(-8,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-8)\\cup (-8,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on these intervals.",
