  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-99050",
      "knowledge_point_id": "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions",
      "problem": "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)={e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}?$",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & ={(12x-{x}^{3})}^{′}{e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2-x)(2+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
